ayed. I\'ve got a good read on what\'s still out there. Landlord has only played a few cards. Teammate played a bomb (5555), suggesting early strength, but I need to decide how to respond. The "Unknown" list is the bible of the game. It shows a tight distribution of potential cards. Specifically, there are no triplets, which makes my Full Houses a near-guarantee to win the trick (unless the Landlord has a Rocket, which I have to consider).\n\nSo here\'s the plan. I\'m going to leverage my hand\'s structure to dominate the game. My cards provide a significant structural advantage (Full House). The safest, most impactful play is to take control and win tricks. By playing these, I can burn a bunch of cards and establish a commanding position. Based on all available evidence, I play 33344. It is the perfect opening gambit. From here, I am setting myself up for a win!\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: I have a strong hand composed of two Full Houses (33344 and 88866) and three high singles (K, A, 2). According to the unknown card count, there are no triplets remaining in the opponents\' hands (max count for any rank is 2). This makes my Full Houses unbeatable by any standard combination; only a Rocket (Red Joker + Black Joker) can beat them. Since the Landlord has only 5 cards left and plays after my teammate, giving up the lead with a single or pair is risky.\n\nReason: Playing the Full House guarantees that I win the trick (unless the Landlord breaks a Rocket, which is also a good outcome). It allows me to maintain the initiative, prevents the Landlord from shedding any cards, and significantly reduces my hand size from 13 to 8. I will follow up with the second Full House and then my high singles to finish or force the highest cards out.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♥3", "♣3", "♦3", "♠4", "♥4"]}'}
